By inspecting the data, we can see that only the four first data points change linearly with time. After 4 seconds, the concentration reaches a plateau. In order to calculate initial velocities, we will use only the first four data points. We can calculate the average initial velocity by doing
\[   \overline{r_0}=\frac{0.14-0.40}{4-1}=-0.086 \text{M/s} \]
We can calculate the instantaneous initial velocity $r_0$ by interpolating the data with a line. You can do this, for example, using the \href{https://www.graphpad.com/quickcalcs/linear1/}{www.graphpad.com/quickcalcs/linear1} web app. The equation of the line is:
 $c=-0.088\cdot t +0.5 $
Hence, the instantaneous initial velocity
\[    r_0= \frac{\text{d}c}{\text{d}t}=-0.086 \text{M/s} \]
